research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-t6-false-statements.log-4701-    87	**What the failure is about.** A compact subset of $\mathbb{R}$ is closed in $\mathbb{R}$, and a subset of $\mathbb{Q}$ that is closed in $\mathbb{R}$ has empty interior in $\mathbb{R}$; so no compact subset of $\mathbb{Q}$ can contain a whole interval's worth of rationals. The two facts pull in opposite directions, and $\mathbb{Q}$ is caught between them precisely because it is dense in $\mathbb{R}$ and is not all of it.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-t6-false-statements.log-4702-    88	
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-t6-false-statements.log-4703-    89	**Heredity does hold in two special cases** and they are proved rather than assumed: along open subspaces of a locally compact Hausdorff space and along closed subspaces of any locally compact space ([[thm-locally-compact-hausdorff-basics]], claim 2). The set $\mathbb{Q}$ is neither open nor closed in $\mathbb{R}$, so it escapes both.
