research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-t7-examples.log-5149-    86	**What the witness does and does not separate.** It separates limit point compactness from countable compactness, and it does so for a reason that is entirely about separation of points: each point has a partner that no open set can distinguish it from, so every point of the space is a limit point of every set containing its partner. Limit point compactness is then satisfied for free.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-t7-examples.log-5150-    87	
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-t7-examples.log-5151-    88	**The space is a product of two very simple spaces**, and each factor contributes one half of the behaviour: the discrete factor supplies the countable open cover with no finite subcover, and the indiscrete factor supplies the partners that make every nonempty set have a limit point.
